WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.071
Episode one: install APEX.

2
00:00:02.071 --> 00:00:07.009
This video assumes only a Linux terminal with internet access.

3
00:00:07.009 --> 00:00:12.975
APEX is tested on Linux with Python 3.12.

4
00:00:12.975 --> 00:00:25.646
APEX is a framework for ensemble-based history matching, forecasting, uncertainty quantification and optimization of subsurface models, with agents that plan, monitor and report on the work.

5
00:00:25.646 --> 00:00:27.380
Status: early development.

6
00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:36.636
First, we check that git is installed.

7
00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:41.114
You need git and uv, the Python package manager APEX uses.

8
00:00:41.114 --> 00:00:45.283
uv also downloads Python 3.12 if your system lacks it.

9
00:00:45.283 --> 00:00:58.524
This command installs uv.

10
00:00:58.524 --> 00:01:12.722
Open a new terminal afterwards (or run source ~/.local/bin/env) so that uv is on your PATH.

11
00:01:12.722 --> 00:01:22.222
Then check it with uv --version.

12
00:01:22.222 --> 00:01:27.513
Next, we clone the public repository into a folder named apex.

13
00:01:27.513 --> 00:01:39.666
APEX's licence is GPL-3.0-or-later.

14
00:01:39.666 --> 00:01:41.373
We move into the new folder.

15
00:01:41.373 --> 00:01:49.156
Every command from now on runs here.

16
00:01:49.156 --> 00:01:51.280
Now we create the environment.

17
00:01:51.280 --> 00:02:02.466
This creates .venv/ inside the folder with APEX, its exact pinned dependencies (uv.lock), the development and documentation tools, and most optional features.

18
00:02:02.466 --> 00:02:13.188
We cut the wait.

19
00:02:13.188 --> 00:02:18.789
Every later command is run from this folder with uv run, which uses that environment.

20
00:02:18.789 --> 00:02:21.029
First, the command-line interface.

21
00:02:21.029 --> 00:02:36.840
Look at the list of commands.

22
00:02:36.840 --> 00:03:03.324
This lists every plugin you can choose (methods, simulators, priors, the agents' skills, ...); [parity], [validated], [stable], [experimental] tags show a status.

23
00:03:03.324 --> 00:03:07.627
To check the installation further, we run the test suite, on 4 cores.

24
00:03:07.627 --> 00:03:17.939
We cut the wait and show the result.

25
00:03:17.939 --> 00:03:22.217
Three optional features are not in the default environment.

26
00:03:22.217 --> 00:03:32.152
This command would add two: co2, CO₂'s equation of state (CoolProp), for monitoring CO₂ storage; and ert, the interface to Equinor's ERT.

27
00:03:32.152 --> 00:03:46.608
Note: each uv sync installs exactly what it is given, so repeat earlier ones when adding another.

28
00:03:46.608 --> 00:03:52.176
Reservoir simulations also need OPM Flow, which is not a Python package.

29
00:03:52.176 --> 00:03:59.291
On Ubuntu 24.04 or later (OPM's packages): these are the commands, as the README lists them.

30
00:03:59.291 --> 00:04:06.405
Note: only OPM Flow (step 5) needs administrator rights, and only for reservoir simulations.

31
00:04:06.405 --> 00:04:22.586
For other systems, see OPM's binary packages.

32
00:04:22.586 --> 00:04:25.688
Once it is installed, this checks OPM Flow.

33
00:04:25.688 --> 00:04:35.424
Episodes six, seven and thirteen need it.

34
00:04:35.424 --> 00:04:40.869
This command serves the documentation; then open http://127.0.0.1:8000 in a browser.

35
00:04:40.869 --> 00:04:53.672
It describes the plugins, the configuration files and the workflows, with a user guide and notebooks.

36
00:04:53.672 --> 00:05:08.197
Later, to update: git pull, then uv sync --locked again, with the same --extra options as before.

37
00:05:08.197 --> 00:05:13.697
Thank you for watching.
